  {
    "id": "why",
    "part": "Start here",
    "title": "Plugins all the way down",
    "tagline": "There is no core you are not allowed to replace.",
    "markdown": "Most agent frameworks give you a brain and let you bolt tools onto it.\n\nPLAIOS does the opposite.\n\n**There is no brain.** There is a host that loads packages, hands each one the others it asked for, and gets out of the way. The model is a package. The memory is a package. The thing that decides which tools to use is a package. Even the chat you are reading this in sits on the same contracts you are about to use.\n\nThat changes what \"writing a plugin\" means.\n\nYou are not writing an extension to someone else's agent. You are writing **a part of the operating system**, on equal footing with every part that shipped with it.\n\n## Three ideas that make it work\n\n- **Contracts, not conventions.** Every kind of component is a TypeScript interface in `@plaios/core/domain`. If your class satisfies it, the host can run it. No base classes, no decorators, no registration calls.\n- **Wiring is declared, not coded.** Your `agent-os.package.yaml` says what you are (`kind: input`) and what you need (`required: [system2]`). The host builds your constructor options from that. You never import another plugin.\n- **Every part has a face.** Each interface ships a page microfrontend, and capabilities can teach the model to draw live components right inside its answers.\n\n## What you will be able to do by the end\n\n- Bring messages in from anywhere: a webhook, a mailbox, a sensor, a cron line.\n- Give the agent a new power, with typed inputs it cannot get wrong.\n- Store your own tables in a database the host scoped just for you.\n- Show a page in Build, and cards the model can place in the chat.\n- Swap the memory, the router or the model for your own.\n- Publish it, and install it from the Store with one click.\n\n> **Tip** Every signature in this guide is copied from the real source. When the guide and the code disagree, the code wins, and the guide has a bug worth reporting.\n\nStart with [One message, start to finish](#journey). It is the map everything else hangs on."
  },
  {
    "id": "journey",
    "part": "Start here",
    "title": "One message, start to finish",
    "tagline": "Follow a single \"turn on the garage light\" through every component it touches.",
    "markdown": "Before you build a part, watch the machine run.\n\nHere is exactly what happens, in order, when any input hands the host a message. This is `AgentRuntime.process`, step by step.\n\n## 1. An input fires\n\nEvery input's `start(listener)` is called once at boot, under a supervisor. When your input calls `listener(message)`, the turn begins. If `start` throws, the host calls `stop()` and tries again one second later. Your input never needs its own retry loop for crashes.\n\n## 2. Memory adds context\n\nBefore anyone thinks, memory is asked two things **in parallel**:\n\n- the last 10 turns of this session (`kinds: [\"working\"]`, `metadata.namespace: \"chat-history\"`)\n- up to 10 relevant long-term memories for this user (`kinds: [\"semantic\", \"episodic\", \"procedural\"]`, only when the message has a `userId`)\n\nThe host rebuilds `message.text` into three blocks: *Relevant memory*, *Conversation history*, *Current request*. The original text stays in `metadata.originalText`.\n\n## 3. The user's turn is saved\n\nRight away, as a `working` entry with id `chat-user-<message id>`. If the request was a \"remember this protocol\" instruction, the host confirms it and the turn ends here.\n\n## 4. The orchestrator decides\n\n`orchestrator.orchestrate(message, outputs)` returns an `OrchestrationDecision`: which capability ids the agent may use, which output gets the answer, what to remember, and whether the model can be skipped entirely.\n\n## 5. Proposed memories are stored\n\nEvery `remember` proposal in the decision is saved for the user, unless an entry with the same content already exists.\n\n## 6. The agent loop does the work\n\n`agentLoop.run(message, { capabilityIds, stream, bypassModel })`. Before the call, the host adds `metadata.deliveryOutputChannel` and, if the chosen output has `instructions`, `metadata.instructions`, so the model knows how to format for its destination.\n\n## 7. Outputs deliver\n\nOne output gets the stream live, token by token. Several outputs get the finished text. Extra copies the orchestrator asked for (`additionalOutputs`) go out too.\n\n## 8. Memory keeps the answer\n\nThe assistant turn is saved. If the orchestrator set `deferMemoryProposal`, it is asked for memories *after* the reply, without making the user wait.\n\n> **Tip** Notice who never talks to whom. Inputs do not know outputs exist. The agent loop never sees memory directly. Every arrow on [the map](#dg-map-title) goes through a contract, which is why you can replace any box.\n\n## Where your plugin sits\n\n| You are building | You are called at step |\n|---|---|\n| An input | 1: you start the turn |\n| Memory | 2, 3, 5 and 8 |\n| An orchestrator | 4 |\n| An agent loop | 6 |\n| A capability (action) | 6, every time the model calls you |\n| An output | 7 |\n| Discovery, models, database, environment | whenever the parts above ask |"
  },
  {
    "id": "anatomy",
    "part": "Start here",
    "title": "Anatomy of a package",
    "tagline": "One npm package, one manifest, any number of interfaces.",
    "markdown": "A plugin is an ordinary npm package with one extra file: `agent-os.package.yaml`. That file is the whole contract between you and the host.\n\n```text package layout\nmy-plugin/\n├─ agent-os.package.yaml   what you are, what you need\n├─ package.json            exports for the class and the web entry\n├─ src/\n│  └─ index.ts             your class(es)\n└─ web/\n   ├─ styles.css           optional, Tailwind-ready\n   └─ action/index.tsx     the page microfrontend (default ./web/<kind>)\n```\n\n## The manifest\n\n```yaml agent-os.package.yaml\nschemaVersion: 1\nid: weather\nname: Weather\nversion: 0.1.0\ndescription: Live forecasts for the agent\ninterfaces:\n  - id: weather.action\n    kind: action\n    name: WeatherCapability\n    className: WeatherCapability\n    description: Answers forecast questions for any city\n    required: [env]\n    config:\n      - key: apiKey\n        env: WEATHER_API_KEY\n        type: secret\n        required: true\n        description: Your weather provider key\n    startup: { when: ifConfigured }\n```\n\nEvery field the host actually reads:\n\n| Field | Required | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `schemaVersion` | yes | Must be `1`. |\n| `id`, `name`, `version` | yes | Non-empty strings. Keep `version` equal to `package.json`. |\n| `interfaces[]` | yes | At least one. Each becomes a component you can enable in Build. |\n| `interfaces[].id` | yes | Unique id, conventionally `<package-id>.<kind>`. |\n| `interfaces[].kind` | yes | One of `env`, `system1`, `system2`, `discovery`, `database`, `memory`, `orchestrator`, `agent`, `input`, `output`, `action`. |\n| `entry` | no | Package export holding the class. Default `.` (your `main`). |\n| `className` | no | The exported class to construct with `new`. |\n| `export` | no | Used as the export name when `className` is absent. Default `.`. |\n| `config[]` | no | Settings with UI, env fallback and defaults. See [Configuration and secrets](#config). |\n| `required`, `optional` | no | Kinds you want handed to your constructor. See [What the host hands you](#injection). |\n| `startup.when` | no | `always`, `ifConfigured` or `manual`. Only `manual` changes behaviour today: the Store does not enable it on install. |\n| `web` | no | Page microfrontend. Defaults to `{ entry: ./web/<kind>, export: default, styles: ./web/styles.css }`. |\n\n> **Careful** `surfaces`, `permissions` and a `capability:` block appear in some manifests, but the host does not read them. Put the real capability manifest on your class.\n\n## How the host finds your code\n\nThe host resolves `entry` and `web.entry` through **`package.json` exports**, exactly like Node does, including `*` wildcards. Both must start with `./` and stay inside your package.\n\n```json package.json\n{\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"main\": \"./dist/src/index.js\",\n  \"exports\": {\n    \".\": { \"types\": \"./dist/src/index.d.ts\", \"import\": \"./dist/src/index.js\" },\n    \"./web/*\": { \"types\": \"./dist/web/*/index.d.ts\", \"import\": \"./dist/web/*/index.js\" },\n    \"./web/styles.css\": \"./web/styles.css\"\n  },\n  \"files\": [\"dist\", \"web\", \"agent-os.package.yaml\"]\n}\n```\n\nThen it constructs you: `new YourClass(options)`. That is the entire lifecycle contract. No registry, no global, no singleton.\n\n## One class, several interfaces\n\nTwo interfaces with the same package and the same `className` share **one instance**. The host constructs it once and reuses it.\n\nThis is how the cron plugin is an `input` (it fires scheduled prompts) *and* a capability (the agent can add jobs) with a single object. A job added by the agent is live immediately, because the listener and the tool are the same object.\n\n## Load order\n\nThe host loads kinds in dependency order, so whatever you `require` already exists:\n\n`env` → `database` → `memory` → `system1` / `system2` → `discovery` → `input` / `output` / `action` → `agent` → `orchestrator`"
  },
  {
    "id": "quickstart",
    "part": "Start here",
    "title": "Your first plugin in five minutes",
    "tagline": "From an empty folder to a tool the agent actually calls.",
    "markdown": "Let's build a real capability: the agent will be able to tell you the weather.\n\n## 1. Scaffold it\n\nFrom the repository root:\n\n```bash terminal\nplaios create action weather\n```\n\nYou get `packages/weather` with a manifest, a class named `WeatherCapability`, a page and a stylesheet. Valid kinds are `input`, `output`, `action`, `agent`, `orchestrator`, `discovery`, `database`, `memory`, `system1`, `system2`, `env`. You can scaffold several at once: `plaios create --kind input,output telegram-clone`.\n\n## 2. Give it a real job\n\n```ts src/action.ts\nimport type {\n  Capability, CapabilityExecutionContext, CapabilityManifest, CapabilityResult,\n  Environment, UIComponentSpec,\n} from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport interface WeatherInput { city: string; days: number | null }\nexport interface WeatherOutput { summary: string; days: Array<{ date: string; high: number; low: number }> }\n\nconst manifest: CapabilityManifest = {\n  id: \"weather.forecast\",\n  version: \"1.0.0\",\n  name: \"get_forecast\",\n  description: \"Gets the weather forecast for a city. Use it whenever the user asks about weather, rain or temperature.\",\n  inputSchema: {\n    type: \"object\",\n    properties: {\n      city: { type: \"string\", description: \"City name, e.g. \\\"Treviso\\\".\" },\n      days: { type: [\"number\", \"null\"], description: \"1 to 7, or null for 3.\" },\n    },\n    required: [\"city\", \"days\"],\n    additionalProperties: false,\n  },\n  tags: [\"weather\", \"forecast\", \"rain\", \"temperature\", \"meteo\"],\n};\n\nexport class WeatherCapability implements Capability<WeatherInput, WeatherOutput> {\n  readonly manifest = manifest;\n  private readonly apiKey: string;\n\n  constructor(options: { env: Environment; apiKey?: string }) {\n    this.apiKey = options.apiKey ?? options.env.getRequired(\"WEATHER_API_KEY\");\n  }\n\n  uiComponents(): readonly UIComponentSpec[] { return []; }\n\n  docs(): string {\n    return \"Use for any weather question. Pass the city as the user wrote it. Days default to 3.\";\n  }\n\n  async execute(input: WeatherInput, context: CapabilityExecutionContext): Promise<CapabilityResult<WeatherOutput>> {\n    if (!input.city?.trim()) return { success: false, error: { code: \"VALIDATION_ERROR\", message: \"city is required\", retryable: false } };\n    const response = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/forecast?q=${encodeURIComponent(input.city)}&days=${input.days ?? 3}&key=${this.apiKey}`, { signal: context.signal });\n    if (!response.ok) return { success: false, error: { code: \"WEATHER_FAILED\", message: `Provider said ${response.status}`, retryable: true } };\n    const days = await response.json() as WeatherOutput[\"days\"];\n    return { success: true, data: { summary: `${days.length} days for ${input.city}`, days } };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> **Tip** Make every property `required` and use `null` for \"not given\". The agent loop sends tools to the model in strict mode, and strict schemas cannot have optional keys.\n\n## 3. Build and publish\n\n```bash terminal\ncd packages/weather\npnpm build\npnpm publish --registry http://<your-registry>:4873\n```\n\n## 4. Install it\n\nOpen **Store**, find `weather`, press **Install**. The host installs the package, checks that its microfrontend resolves, adds a component to `plaios.yaml` with your config defaults, and reloads.\n\nSet `WEATHER_API_KEY` in `.env` (or on the component page), and ask the chat: *\"Will it rain in Treviso tomorrow?\"*\n\nThat's it. No restart scripts, no code in the host, no prompt editing. The orchestrator finds you through your name, description and tags, and the agent loop calls you with typed arguments."
  },
  {
    "id": "input",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Inputs",
    "tagline": "Anything that can produce a sentence can talk to your agent.",
    "kinds": [
      "input"
    ],
    "markdown": "Telegram, the HTTP API, cron jobs, the voice line and this very chat are all inputs. An input has exactly one job: **notice something and hand the host a message**.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface InputInterface {\n  readonly channel: InputChannel;           // \"telegram\", \"cron\", \"web\"...\n  start(listener: InputListener): Promise<void>;\n  stop(): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nexport type InputListener = (message: InputMessage) => void | Promise<void>;\n\nexport interface InputMessage {\n  id: string;\n  channel: InputChannel;\n  sessionId: string;              // groups turns into a conversation\n  userId?: string;                // enables long-term memory for this person\n  text: string;\n  attachments?: readonly InputAttachment[];   // { type: \"image\"; path; mediaType? }\n  createdAt: Date;\n  metadata?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n}\n```\n\n## A complete webhook input\n\n```ts src/input.ts\nimport { createServer, type Server } from \"node:http\";\nimport { randomUUID } from \"node:crypto\";\nimport type { InputInterface, InputListener } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport class WebhookInput implements InputInterface {\n  readonly channel = \"webhook\";\n  private server?: Server;\n\n  constructor(private readonly options: { port: number }) {}\n\n  async start(listener: InputListener): Promise<void> {\n    this.server = createServer(async (request, response) => {\n      let body = \"\";\n      for await (const chunk of request) body += chunk;\n      const { text, sender } = JSON.parse(body) as { text: string; sender: string };\n      response.writeHead(202).end();\n      await listener({\n        id: randomUUID(),\n        channel: this.channel,\n        sessionId: `webhook:${sender}`,\n        userId: sender,\n        text,\n        createdAt: new Date(),\n      });\n    });\n    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => this.server!.listen(this.options.port, resolve));\n  }\n\n  async stop(): Promise<void> {\n    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => this.server ? this.server.close(() => resolve()) : resolve());\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## How it plugs in\n\n- **`start` is called once**, when the runtime boots, under a supervisor. If it throws, the host calls `stop()` and starts you again after one second.\n- `start` may resolve right away (like above, a server keeps running) or stay pending (a polling loop). Either works.\n- **`stop` is called on every reload**: saving config, installing a package, toggling a component. Release ports, timers and sockets there, or the next `start` will collide with your own ghost.\n- Your `channel` is your name in routing. The orchestrator replies to the input's channel by default, so an input usually ships with an output of the same channel.\n\n## Choosing ids that make memory work\n\n- **`sessionId`** decides which history the model sees. One per chat, per thread, per caller.\n- **`userId`** decides whose long-term memory is recalled. Leave it out and the agent remembers the conversation but not the person.\n- **`metadata.preferredOutputChannel`** is a hint some orchestrators honour (the web chat sets `\"web\"`).\n\n> **Tip** Need to know when a reply goes out? Ship an output with the same `channel`. Inputs never see replies, on purpose: it keeps every input usable with any output.\n\n> **Careful** Two instances of your input on the same port means a crash loop. If people may enable it twice, make the port configurable."
  },
  {
    "id": "output",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Outputs",
    "tagline": "The last mile, and the only part that knows what \"sent\" means.",
    "kinds": [
      "output"
    ],
    "markdown": "An output receives the answer and delivers it. That is all. But two optional fields make it smarter than it looks.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport type OutputContent = string | AsyncIterable<string>;\n\nexport interface OutputInterface {\n  readonly channel: OutputChannel;\n  /** Routing guidance: tells an orchestrator when to pick this output. */\n  readonly description?: string;\n  /** Formatting guidance: handed to the model when this output is chosen. */\n  readonly instructions?: string;\n  write(content: OutputContent): Promise<void>;\n}\n```\n\n## Streaming, or not\n\n`write` gets either a **string** or an **async iterable of chunks**:\n\n- When the answer goes to **one** output, you get the live stream. Render it as it arrives.\n- When it goes to **several**, the host buffers it and every output gets the finished string.\n\nHandle both, always:\n\n```ts src/output.ts\nimport type { OutputContent, OutputInterface } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport class SlackOutput implements OutputInterface {\n  readonly channel = \"slack\";\n  readonly description = \"Posts to the team's #agent channel. Use when the user says 'tell the team' or 'post on Slack'.\";\n  readonly instructions = \"Slack message: short, Slack markdown (*bold*, bullet lists), no headings.\";\n\n  constructor(private readonly options: { webhookUrl: string }) {}\n\n  async write(content: OutputContent): Promise<void> {\n    const text = typeof content === \"string\" ? content : await collect(content);\n    await fetch(this.options.webhookUrl, { method: \"POST\", headers: { \"content-type\": \"application/json\" }, body: JSON.stringify({ text }) });\n  }\n}\n\nasync function collect(stream: AsyncIterable<string>): Promise<string> {\n  let text = \"\";\n  for await (const chunk of stream) text += chunk;\n  return text;\n}\n```\n\n## Why `description` and `instructions` matter\n\n- **`description`** is how \"send this to my team\" ends up in Slack instead of the chat it was typed in. The orchestrator reads every output's description when it picks `outputChannel` and any `additionalOutputs`.\n- **`instructions`** reaches the model as `metadata.instructions` for this turn only. Telegram can ask for short plain text while the web chat asks for rich components, from the very same agent.\n\n> **Tip** If the user wrote from Telegram but the answer goes to Slack, the host tells the model so in a \"Delivery context\" note, so it does not say \"here you go\" to the wrong room.\n\n> **Careful** If an orchestrator names an `outputChannel` that no installed output has, the turn fails. Your channel string is an API: never rename it casually."
  },
  {
    "id": "action",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Capabilities and actions",
    "tagline": "Give the agent a new power, with inputs it cannot get wrong.",
    "kinds": [
      "action"
    ],
    "markdown": "A capability is a typed function the model can call. Home Assistant, SSH, web search, cron management and memory retrieval are all capabilities. It's where most plugins start, and where most of the magic happens.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface Capability<TInput = unknown, TOutput = unknown> {\n  readonly manifest: CapabilityManifest;\n  initialize?(): Promise<void>;\n  execute(input: TInput, context: CapabilityExecutionContext): Promise<CapabilityResult<TOutput>>;\n  /** Chat components the model may write into an answer. [] when none. */\n  uiComponents(): readonly UIComponentSpec[];\n  /** Markdown the agent reads on demand before using you. */\n  docs(): string;\n}\n\nexport interface CapabilityManifest {\n  id: string;                   // stable id, e.g. \"web.search\"\n  name: string;                 // the function name the model sees: search_web\n  description: string;          // when to use it, in one or two sentences\n  inputSchema: JsonSchema;      // strict JSON Schema for the arguments\n  version?: string;\n  outputSchema?: JsonSchema;\n  permissions?: readonly string[];\n  tags?: readonly string[];     // keywords discovery matches on\n  execution?: { timeoutMs?: number; idempotent?: boolean };\n  examples?: ReadonlyArray<{ request: string; arguments?: unknown }>;\n}\n\nexport type CapabilityResult<T = unknown> =\n  | { success: true; data: T }\n  | { success: false; error: { code: string; message: string; retryable?: boolean; details?: unknown } };\n\nexport interface CapabilityExecutionContext {\n  runId: string; callId: string;\n  sessionId?: string; userId?: string;\n  startedAt: Date;\n  signal?: AbortSignal;          // pass it to fetch: cancelled turns stop your work\n  metadata?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n}\n```\n\n## Who reads what\n\nEach field of your manifest has exactly one audience. Write each for that reader:\n\n| Field | Read by | Write it as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name`, `description`, `tags` | the orchestrator, to pick tools | \"Use it when...\" plus every word a user might say |\n| `name`, `description`, `inputSchema` | the model, as a function definition | precise argument descriptions with an example |\n| `tags`, `examples` | discovery, for keyword search | synonyms, other languages, nouns |\n| `docs()` | the model, **on demand**, via `read_capability_docs` | the manual: when not to use it, limits, examples |\n| `uiComponents()` | the model, in the web chat prompt | see [Chat components](#chat-components) |\n\n## The result is a conversation\n\n`data` goes back to the model as the function result. Make it **small and meaningful**:\n\n- Return what the model needs to answer, not your raw API payload. A `summary` string is a gift.\n- Return `success: false` with a stable `code` and a human `message`. Set `retryable: true` only if trying again can actually help.\n- Never throw for expected failures. Throwing works, but the model gets a generic error instead of your helpful one.\n\n## The direct path: skipping the model\n\nIf your capability can act on raw user text by itself (like the Jev Home Assistant action, which turns \"spegni la luce in cucina\" into a service call), tag it `direct`. When an orchestrator is confident only you are needed, it sets `bypassModel`, and the agent loop calls `execute` with the raw text and history, then returns your `summary` as the answer. A sub-second reply, no generative model involved.\n\n## Being a capability without being an action\n\nThe host registers **every** component instance that has a `manifest` and an `execute`, whatever its kind. The cron input and the graph memory are capabilities too. So if your memory, input or output would benefit from a tool (\"list my scheduled jobs\"), just implement `Capability` on the same class.\n\n> **Tip** Strict schemas need every property in `required`. Model \"optional\" as `type: [\"string\", \"null\"]` and treat `null` as \"not given\". Models handle this perfectly.\n\n> **Careful** The host never calls `initialize()` today. Do your setup lazily on the first `execute`, or in the constructor if it cannot fail."
  },
  {
    "id": "memory",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Memory",
    "tagline": "The only component that is called before, during and after every turn.",
    "kinds": [
      "memory"
    ],
    "markdown": "Memory is the most connected box on the map. Replace it, and you change what the agent knows about everyone it has ever talked to.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport type MemoryKind = \"working\" | \"semantic\" | \"episodic\" | \"procedural\";\n\nexport interface MemoryEntry<T = unknown> {\n  id: string; kind: MemoryKind; content: T; createdAt: Date;\n  addedMemoryDate?: Date; factDate?: Date; updatedAt?: Date;\n  userId?: string; sessionId?: string;\n  importance?: number; confidence?: number;\n  metadata?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n}\n\nexport interface MemoryQuery {\n  text?: string; kinds?: readonly MemoryKind[];\n  userId?: string; sessionId?: string;\n  limit?: number; minImportance?: number;\n  metadata?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n  order?: \"oldest\" | \"newest\";\n}\n\nexport interface Memory {\n  remember(entry: MemoryEntry): Promise<void>;\n  get(id: string): Promise<MemoryEntry | undefined>;\n  recall(query?: MemoryQuery): Promise<readonly MemoryEntry[]>;\n  forget(id: string): Promise<boolean>;\n  clear(query?: MemoryQuery): Promise<number>;\n  close?(): Promise<void>;\n}\n```\n\n## Exactly how the host uses you\n\n| When | Call | Query or entry |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before thinking | `recall` | `{ sessionId, kinds: [\"working\"], metadata: { namespace: \"chat-history\" }, limit: 10, order: \"newest\" }` |\n| Before thinking, in parallel | `recall` | `{ text, kinds: [\"semantic\", \"episodic\", \"procedural\"], userId, limit: 10, order: \"newest\" }` (only with a `userId`) |\n| User turn | `remember` | `kind: \"working\"`, id `chat-user-<id>`, `metadata: { namespace: \"chat-history\", role: \"user\", channel }` |\n| After the decision | `remember` | each proposal, id `user-memory-<uuid>`, `metadata: { namespace: \"user-memory\", reason, sourceChannel }` |\n| After the answer | `remember` | `kind: \"working\"`, `role: \"assistant\"` |\n\nSo the minimum viable memory must:\n\n1. **Filter by `metadata`** (exact match on the keys given). Chat history depends on it.\n2. **Honour `order` and `limit`**. The host reverses \"newest 10\" into chronological history.\n3. **Rank by `text`** when it is present. This is your semantic search: keywords, embeddings, a graph walk, your choice.\n\n## Bonus hooks\n\n- If your memory has a `link(...)` method, the host calls it to connect a new turn to the memories that were recalled for it. The graph memory uses this to grow edges.\n- Expose a capability on the same class (like `memory.retrieval`) and the agent can search you *mid-thought*, with follow-up queries.\n\n## Storage\n\nMemory loads right after the database, so `options.database` is ready in your constructor. Create your tables on first use. See [The database](#database).\n\n> **Tip** `content` is `unknown` on purpose. The default memory stores strings, but a graph memory stores nodes. Whatever you store, `recall` must give back entries the host can print: put readable text in `content` or a `metadata.text`.\n\n> **Careful** Only the **first** memory component is used. Installing a second one does not merge them; it is silently ignored."
  },
  {
    "id": "orchestrator",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Orchestrators",
    "tagline": "The decision before the thinking: which tools, which destination, what to remember.",
    "kinds": [
      "orchestrator"
    ],
    "markdown": "The orchestrator sees the message once, before the agent loop, and writes the plan. A great orchestrator makes a cheap model feel brilliant, because the model only ever sees the five tools that matter.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface Orchestrator {\n  orchestrate(\n    message: InputMessage,\n    outputs: readonly OutputInterface[],\n    options?: OrchestratorOptions,\n  ): Promise<OrchestrationDecision>;\n  proposeMemory?(message: InputMessage, options?: OrchestratorOptions): Promise<readonly MemoryProposal[]>;\n}\n\nexport interface OrchestrationDecision {\n  capabilityIds: readonly string[];      // what the agent loop may use\n  outputChannel: OutputChannel;          // must be one of `outputs`\n  additionalOutputs?: readonly { outputChannel: OutputChannel; content: \"response\" | \"text\"; text?: string }[];\n  memoryProposals?: readonly MemoryProposal[];\n  deferMemoryProposal?: boolean;         // call proposeMemory after replying\n  bypassModel?: boolean;                 // let a `direct` capability answer alone\n  reason?: string;\n}\n\nexport type MemoryProposal =\n  | { operation: \"remember\"; kind: MemoryKind; content: unknown; reason: string; confidence?: number; factDate?: Date }\n  | { operation: \"forget\"; memoryId: string; reason: string };\n```\n\n## What you are given\n\nOrchestrators always receive `model` (the System 2 instance) and `capabilityDiscovery` in their options, without asking. Add `system1` by declaring it:\n\n```yaml agent-os.package.yaml\n    kind: orchestrator\n    optional: [system1]   # options.system1, when one is installed\n```\n\n## The two shipped strategies\n\n- **Default orchestrator**: one forced function call to the main model (`route_agent_message`) with every capability's id, name, description and tags. Accurate, about one model round trip.\n- **Jev orchestrator**: asks System 1 one yes/no question per capability plus `memory`, `multiStep` and `output`, keeps everything scoring 0.5 or more, and sets `bypassModel` when one `direct` capability scores 0.7 or more on a single-step request. Fast, and it falls back to System 2 when System 1 fails.\n\n## A tiny one to learn from\n\n```ts src/orchestrator.ts\nimport type { CapabilityDiscovery, InputMessage, OrchestrationDecision, Orchestrator, OutputInterface } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport class KeywordOrchestrator implements Orchestrator {\n  constructor(private readonly options: { capabilityDiscovery: CapabilityDiscovery }) {}\n\n  async orchestrate(message: InputMessage, outputs: readonly OutputInterface[]): Promise<OrchestrationDecision> {\n    const text = String(message.metadata?.originalText ?? message.text);\n    const found = await this.options.capabilityDiscovery.discover({ text, limit: 8 });\n    const reply = outputs.find((output) => output.channel === message.channel) ?? outputs[0]!;\n    return { capabilityIds: found.map((capability) => capability.id), outputChannel: reply.channel, reason: \"keyword match\" };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> **Tip** Route on `metadata.originalText`. By the time you run, `message.text` also contains the memory and history blocks, which will confuse keyword matching.\n\n> **Careful** `outputChannel` must exist. When unsure, fall back to the output whose `channel` equals `message.channel`, then to `outputs[0]`. Never invent a channel."
  },
  {
    "id": "agent",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "The agent loop",
    "tagline": "Where the model meets the tools, step after step.",
    "kinds": [
      "agent"
    ],
    "markdown": "The agent loop takes the plan and makes it happen: it loads the chosen capabilities, hands them to the model as functions, runs every call the model makes, and returns the final answer. Write your own when you want a different way of thinking, like planning first, voting between models, or a hard budget.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface AgentLoop {\n  run(message: InputMessage, options: AgentLoopOptions): Promise<AIProcessResult>;\n}\n\nexport interface AgentLoopOptions {\n  capabilityIds: readonly string[];   // from the orchestrator\n  stream: boolean;                    // false when AI_STREAM=false\n  signal?: AbortSignal;\n  onEvent?: AgentLoopEventHandler;    // feeds telemetry\n  bypassModel?: boolean;              // run the capability directly\n}\n\nexport type AIProcessResult =\n  | { type: \"text\"; text: string }\n  | { type: \"stream\"; stream: AsyncIterable<string> };\n```\n\nLike orchestrators, agent loops always receive `model` and `capabilityDiscovery` in their options.\n\n## The default loop, in 20 lines\n\n```ts src/agent.ts\nimport type { AgentLoop, AgentLoopOptions, AIExecutableFunctionDefinition, AIProcessResult, CapabilityDiscovery, InputMessage, System2 } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport class SimpleAgentLoop implements AgentLoop {\n  constructor(private readonly options: { model: System2; capabilityDiscovery: CapabilityDiscovery }) {}\n\n  async run(message: InputMessage, options: AgentLoopOptions): Promise<AIProcessResult> {\n    const ids = [\"memory.retrieval\", \"agent-os.docs\", ...options.capabilityIds];\n    const capabilities = (await Promise.all([...new Set(ids)].map((id) => this.options.capabilityDiscovery.get(id)))).filter((item) => item !== undefined);\n    const functions: AIExecutableFunctionDefinition[] = capabilities.map((capability) => ({\n      name: capability.manifest.name,\n      description: capability.manifest.description,\n      inputSchema: capability.manifest.inputSchema,\n      strict: true,\n      execute: (args, context) => capability.execute(args, { runId: message.id, callId: context.callId, sessionId: message.sessionId, userId: message.userId, startedAt: new Date(), signal: context.signal }),\n    }));\n    const instructions = [\"You are PLAIOS.\", String(message.metadata?.instructions ?? \"\")].join(\"\\n\");\n    return this.options.model.processWithFunctions(message.text, functions, { stream: options.stream, instructions, signal: options.signal, maxSteps: 25 });\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Things the host expects you to respect\n\n- **`metadata.instructions`** carries the chosen output's formatting rules, including the full chat component library in the web chat. Append it to your system instructions or the web chat cannot draw cards.\n- **Always load `memory.retrieval` and `agent-os.docs`** (the default loop's `alwaysCapabilityIds`). The first lets the model dig deeper into memory; the second lets it read any capability's manual.\n- **Emit `onEvent`** (`capability.started`, `capability.completed`, `capability.failed`) if you want your runs to show in telemetry.\n- **Images**: when `message.attachments` holds images, pass `{ text, images }` as the `AIInput` so vision models can see them.\n\n> **Tip** `bypassModel` means \"the orchestrator is sure\". The default loop then runs each chosen capability with the raw user text and history, and joins their `summary` fields into the answer. No model call at all: that is the sub-second path."
  },
  {
    "id": "discovery",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "Discovery",
    "tagline": "The phone book of every capability, and the reason you never register anything by hand.",
    "kinds": [
      "discovery"
    ],
    "markdown": "At boot, after every component is constructed, the host walks all of them and calls `discovery.register(instance)` for each one that is a capability. Then the orchestrator asks discovery what exists, and the agent loop asks it for the chosen ones by id.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface CapabilityDiscovery {\n  register(capability: Capability): Promise<void>;\n  get(id: string, version?: string): Promise<Capability | undefined>;\n  discover(query?: CapabilityDiscoveryQuery): Promise<readonly CapabilityManifest[]>;\n}\n\nexport interface CapabilityDiscoveryQuery {\n  text?: string;\n  tags?: readonly string[];\n  limit?: number;\n}\n```\n\n## What the default one does\n\n`discovery-memory` keeps capabilities in memory and scores `discover({ text })` by keyword overlap with each manifest's name, description, `tags`, `examples` and input schema. That is why tags matter: `[\"weather\", \"meteo\", \"pioggia\"]` lets an Italian request find an English-named tool.\n\n## Writing your own\n\nEmbeddings, usage-based ranking, per-user allowlists: discovery is the place.\n\n- `discover()` with no query should return **everything**. Orchestrators use it to list all capabilities.\n- `get(id)` must be fast and exact; the agent loop calls it for every tool on every turn.\n- Every reload builds a fresh discovery instance and registers everything again, so keep `register` cheap and do not rely on state surviving a reload.\n\n> **Tip** A plugin of any kind can ask for discovery with `required: [discovery]`. It then receives `capabilityDiscovery` and `model` in its options, which is how a capability can call *other* capabilities."
  },
  {
    "id": "models",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "System 1 and System 2",
    "tagline": "A fast mind for judgments, a slow mind for words. Use both.",
    "kinds": [
      "system1",
      "system2"
    ],
    "markdown": "PLAIOS splits the model in two, like the psychology it is named after.\n\n- **System 1** answers typed questions instantly: yes/no as a probability, a choice between labels, a score. It cannot write a sentence, and that is the point.\n- **System 2** is the generative model. It writes, reasons and calls tools, and it can answer the same typed questions, just slower.\n\n## The contracts\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport type SystemQuestion =\n  | { type: \"noul\"; instructions?: string; criteria?: { true?: unknown; false?: unknown } | null }\n  | { type: \"choice\"; instructions?: string; criteria: Record<string, unknown> }\n  | { type: \"score\"; instructions?: string; criteria: readonly unknown[] };\n\nexport type SystemQuestions = Record<string, SystemQuestion>;\nexport interface SystemAnswer { type?: string; choice?: string; noul?: number; score?: number }\n\nexport interface System1 {\n  readonly model: string;\n  ask(state: Record<string, unknown>, questions: SystemQuestions, options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<Record<string, SystemAnswer>>;\n}\n\nexport interface System2 extends AIProvider {\n  ask(state: Record<string, unknown>, questions: SystemQuestions, options?: { signal?: AbortSignal }): Promise<Record<string, SystemAnswer>>;\n}\n\nexport interface AIProvider {\n  readonly provider: string; readonly model: string; readonly settings: Readonly<AIModelSettings>;\n  processInput(input: AIInput, options?: AIProcessOptions): Promise<AIProcessResult>;\n  functionCall<T>(input: string, definition: AIFunctionDefinition<T>, options?: Omit<AIProcessOptions, \"stream\">): Promise<AIFunctionCallResult<T>>;\n  processWithFunctions(input: AIInput, definitions: readonly AIExecutableFunctionDefinition[], options?: AIFunctionProcessOptions): Promise<AIProcessResult>;\n}\n```\n\n## Using them from any plugin\n\nDeclare what you need and it appears in your options:\n\n```yaml agent-os.package.yaml\n    required: [system2]\n    optional: [system1]\n```\n\n```ts src/action.ts\nconst answers = await (this.options.system1 ?? this.options.system2).ask(\n  { request: text, devices: [\"kitchen light\", \"garage door\"] },\n  {\n    isCommand: { type: \"noul\", instructions: \"Is the user asking to change a device?\" },\n    device: { type: \"choice\", instructions: \"Which device?\", criteria: { kitchen: \"kitchen light\", garage: \"garage door\" } },\n  },\n);\nif ((answers.isCommand?.noul ?? 0) > 0.7) await this.toggle(answers.device?.choice);\n```\n\nThat fallback line is the whole philosophy. Try the fast mind, use the slow one when it is missing. Both speak the same question format, so your code does not care which one answered.\n\n## Picking an instance\n\nWith several models installed, set `system1Instance` or `system2Instance` in your component config to an instance id. Otherwise the host gives you the one with `config.role: routing`, or the first.\n\n## Writing a provider\n\nImplement `AIProvider` plus `ask` and you are a System 2: Codex, OpenRouter or a local model all fit. `processWithFunctions` owns the tool loop: call the model, run each `execute` it asks for, feed results back, stop after `maxSteps`, and stream the final text when `stream` is true."
  },
  {
    "id": "database",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "The database",
    "tagline": "Your own tables, in a namespace nobody else can touch.",
    "kinds": [
      "database"
    ],
    "markdown": "Every component gets a database. Not a connection string, not a shared pool: a **scoped view** of the installed database provider, named after your instance. Your `searches` table and someone else's `searches` table never meet.\n\n## You already have it\n\nIf a database component is installed, every plugin receives `options.database`, already scoped. The namespace is your instance id with `:` replaced by `-`, so `action:web-search` gets `action-web-search`. List `database` under `optional` to say you use it.\n\n```yaml agent-os.package.yaml\n    optional: [database]\n```\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface Database {\n  readonly namespace: string;\n  init(structure: DatabaseStructure): Promise<void>;\n  add<T extends DatabaseRecord>(table: string, value: T): Promise<T>;\n  get<T extends DatabaseRecord>(table: string, id: DatabaseKey): Promise<T | undefined>;\n  get<T extends DatabaseRecord>(table: string, query?: DatabaseQuery): Promise<readonly T[]>;\n  set<T extends DatabaseRecord>(table: string, id: DatabaseKey, value: T): Promise<T>;\n  update<T extends DatabaseRecord>(table: string, id: DatabaseKey, patch: Partial<T>): Promise<T | undefined>;\n  delete(table: string, target: DatabaseKey | DatabaseQuery): Promise<number>;\n}\n\nexport interface DatabaseQuery {\n  where?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;\n  orderBy?: readonly { field: string; direction?: \"asc\" | \"desc\" }[];\n  limit?: number;\n  offset?: number;\n}\n\n// column types: \"string\" | \"number\" | \"boolean\" | \"datetime\" | \"json\"\n```\n\n## A real example: keep the last 200 searches\n\nThis is the pattern the Perplexity plugin uses for its `WebSources` cards:\n\n```ts src/search-store.ts\nconst TABLE = \"searches\";\n\nexport class SearchStore {\n  private ready?: Promise<void>;\n  constructor(private readonly database: Database) {}\n\n  private init() {\n    this.ready ??= this.database.init({\n      tables: {\n        [TABLE]: {\n          primaryKey: \"id\",\n          columns: {\n            id: { type: \"string\", required: true },\n            query: { type: \"string\", required: true },\n            results: { type: \"json\", required: true },\n            createdAt: { type: \"datetime\", required: true },\n          },\n          indexes: [{ columns: [\"createdAt\"] }],\n        },\n      },\n    });\n    return this.ready;\n  }\n\n  async save(search: StoredSearch) {\n    await this.init();\n    await this.database.set(TABLE, search.id, { ...search });\n    const stale = await this.database.get<StoredSearch>(TABLE, { orderBy: [{ field: \"createdAt\", direction: \"desc\" }], offset: 200 });\n    await Promise.all(stale.map((row) => this.database.delete(TABLE, row.id)));\n  }\n\n  async find(id: string) {\n    await this.init();\n    return this.database.get<StoredSearch>(TABLE, id);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThree habits worth stealing:\n\n- **Init lazily, once**: keep the `init` promise and await it everywhere. It is idempotent, so calling it on every boot is fine.\n- **`set` for upserts, `add` for inserts** that must not overwrite.\n- **Trim as you write**: a `delete` with a query keeps the table bounded forever.\n\n## Writing a provider\n\nA database component implements `DatabaseProvider`:\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface DatabaseProvider {\n  scope(namespace: string): Database;\n  close?(): Promise<void>;\n}\n```\n\nThe shipped one is SQLite through `node:sqlite`. A Postgres provider is a `scope()` that prefixes table names, or uses one schema per namespace.\n\n> **Tip** Plan for no database. Wrap your store so it falls back to an in-memory `Map` when `options.database` is undefined. Your plugin then still works on a minimal install, just without persistence."
  },
  {
    "id": "env",
    "part": "The components",
    "title": "The environment",
    "tagline": "Where configuration and secrets come from, before anything else exists.",
    "kinds": [
      "env"
    ],
    "markdown": "Environment components load first, because everything else reads from them. The shipped ones read `process.env`, a `.env` file, a fixed map, and a **composite** that layers the others.\n\n## The contract\n\n```ts @plaios/core/domain\nexport interface Environment {\n  get(key: string): string | undefined;\n  getRequired(key: string): string;               // throws when missing\n  getOrDefault(key: string, fallback: string): string;\n  has(key: string): boolean;\n  getAll(): Record<string, string>;\n}\n```\n\n## How you receive it\n\nEvery component gets `options.env`: the composite environment when one is installed, otherwise the first environment. Declaring `required: [env]` documents that you depend on it.\n\nThe host also uses it for you: a config field with `env: WEATHER_API_KEY` is filled from `env.get(\"WEATHER_API_KEY\")`, then `process.env`, before your constructor runs. See [Configuration and secrets](#config).\n\n## Writing one\n\nA vault-backed environment is the classic case:\n\n```ts src/env.ts\nimport type { Environment } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport class VaultEnvironment implements Environment {\n  private readonly values: Record<string, string>;\n  constructor(options: { values?: Record<string, string> }) { this.values = options.values ?? {}; }\n  get(key: string) { return this.values[key]; }\n  has(key: string) { return key in this.values; }\n  getRequired(key: string) { const value = this.get(key); if (value === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing ${key}`); return value; }\n  getOrDefault(key: string, fallback: string) { return this.get(key) ?? fallback; }\n  getAll() { return { ...this.values }; }\n}\n```\n\n> **Careful** Your constructor is synchronous and runs at boot. If your secrets live behind a network call, load them into memory before the host starts, or resolve lazily inside `get`.\n\n> **Careful** The composite environment receives `options.providers`, every other environment instance. Only the class named `CompositeEnvironment` gets that list."
  },
  {
    "id": "injection",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "What the host hands your constructor",
    "tagline": "One options object, assembled from your manifest. Every key, and exactly when you get it.",
    "markdown": "You never import another plugin. You **declare**, and the host **injects**. Everything arrives in the single object passed to `new YourClass(options)`.\n\n## Every key\n\n| Key | You get it when | What it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cwd`, `workingDirectory` | always | the folder holding `plaios.yaml` |\n| `env` | an environment is installed | the composite environment, else the first one |\n| `memory` | a memory is installed | the first memory component |\n| `database` | a database is installed | a view scoped to your instance id (`action:foo` → `action-foo`) |\n| `model` | you are an `orchestrator` or `agent`, or list `discovery` | a System 2 instance |\n| `capabilityDiscovery` | same as `model` | the capability registry |\n| `system1`, `system2` | you list them in `required` or `optional` | the chosen model instance |\n| `providers` | your class is `CompositeEnvironment` | every other environment |\n| *your config keys* | always | resolved config values, see [Configuration and secrets](#config) |\n\n## Which model you get\n\nWith several models, the host picks by your component config `system1Instance` / `system2Instance` (an instance id), then the one whose config has `role: routing`, then the first.\n\n## A typed options interface\n\nSpell it out, so a missing dependency is a type error and not a runtime surprise:\n\n```ts src/action.ts\nimport type { Database, Environment, Memory, System1, System2 } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport interface HomeLightsOptions {\n  env: Environment;\n  system2: System2;          // required: [system2]\n  system1?: System1;         // optional: [system1]\n  database?: Database;       // optional: [database]\n  memory?: Memory;\n  baseUrl: string;           // config key\n  timeoutMs?: number;        // config key with a default\n}\n```\n\n> **Tip** `required` versus `optional` is documentation for the person installing you. Injection works the same for both: if the kind is installed, you get it. Check optional ones for `undefined`.\n\n> **Careful** Two interfaces that share a `className` share one instance, built with the **first** interface's options. Give both interfaces the same `required` and `optional` so that first options object has everything."
  },
  {
    "id": "config",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Configuration and secrets",
    "tagline": "Declare a field once. Get a settings form, an env fallback and a default for free.",
    "markdown": "Every `config` entry in your manifest becomes three things at once: a field on your component page, a value in your constructor options, and a line in the docs the agent can read.\n\n```yaml agent-os.package.yaml\n    config:\n      - key: baseUrl\n        env: HOME_ASSISTANT_URL\n        type: string\n        required: true\n        description: Home Assistant origin, e.g. http://homeassistant.local:8123\n      - key: token\n        env: HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN\n        type: secret\n        required: true\n        description: Long-lived access token\n      - key: timeoutMs\n        type: number\n        default: 10000\n        description: Request timeout in milliseconds\n```\n\n## Field shape\n\n| Property | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `key` | the option name your constructor receives |\n| `type` | `string`, `number`, `boolean` or `secret` |\n| `env` | environment variable to fall back on |\n| `default` | used when nothing else is set; written into `plaios.yaml` on install |\n| `required` | `true` means the component will not start without a value |\n| `description` | shown under the field, and to the agent |\n\n## How a value is resolved\n\nFor each field, in this order:\n\n1. the value saved on the component page (in `plaios.yaml`)\n2. the environment variable named by `env`, read through the environment component, then `process.env`\n3. `default`\n4. still empty and `required: true`: the component does not start, and Build shows *Missing required config \"token\" (HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN)*\n\nThen `number` values go through `Number()` and `boolean` values accept `true`/`false`/`\"1\"`/`\"0\"`. Anything else is rejected.\n\n## Secrets stay secret\n\n- Put secrets in `.env` and reference them with `env:`. The page then shows `$HOME_ASSISTANT_TOKEN` instead of a value.\n- `read_capability_docs` shows a `secret` field only as *set* or *not set*, never the value.\n- Saving the page rewrites the whole config object and reloads the runtime, so your constructor always sees a consistent set.\n\n> **Careful** A value typed on the page is stored as entered and overrides the coerced one. So a `number` saved as `\"10\"` reaches you as a string. Coerce defensively: `Math.trunc(Number(options.timeoutMs ?? 10_000))`."
  },
  {
    "id": "pages",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Your page and the data bridge",
    "tagline": "A React page in Build that can call your class like a local object.",
    "markdown": "Every interface has a page. It is required: the Store refuses to install a package whose page cannot be found, and Build flags one that goes missing. The good news is that the page can do almost anything, because of the **data bridge**.\n\n## The page\n\nThe default export of your web entry (`./web/<kind>` by default) is a React component:\n\n```ts @plaios/microfrontend\nexport interface MicrofrontendProps<TData = unknown> {\n  component: ComponentInstance;   // instanceId, kind, id, version, config, fields\n  data?: TData;                   // whatever your class's microfrontendData() returned\n  onSaveConfig?: (config: Record<string, unknown>) => void | Promise<void>;\n}\n```\n\n`@plaios/microfrontend` gives you the building blocks the shipped plugins use: `MicrofrontendFrame`, `ConfigurationEditor`, `InstanceSummary`, `Card`, `Badge`, `Button`, `Input`, `Label`, `Textarea`, and `cn`. The two-line page is a real, useful page:\n\n```tsx web/action/index.tsx\nimport { CapabilityMicrofrontend, type MicrofrontendProps } from \"@plaios/microfrontend\";\n\nexport default function WeatherPage(props: MicrofrontendProps) {\n  return <CapabilityMicrofrontend {...props} />;   // config form + instance summary\n}\n```\n\n## The data bridge\n\nGive your class a `microfrontendData()` method, and whatever it returns arrives as `data` in your page. **Functions inside it become callable from the browser.**\n\n```ts src/action.ts\nmicrofrontendData() {\n  return {\n    connected: this.connected,\n    devices: this.devices,                    // plain data, serialized as JSON\n    queries: {\n      snapshot: (entityId: string) => this.cameraSnapshot(entityId),   // read-only\n    },\n    actions: {\n      control: async (input: ControlInput) => { await this.control(input); return this.dashboard(); },\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n```tsx web/action/index.tsx\nexport default function HomePage({ data }: MicrofrontendProps<HomeData>) {\n  return <button onClick={() => data?.actions.control({ entityId: \"light.desk\", action: \"toggle\" })}>Toggle</button>;\n}\n```\n\nWhat happens under the hood:\n\n- Each function is replaced by a marker with its path (`actions.control`). In the browser, the marker becomes an `async` function that posts `{ path, args }` to `/api/components/<instanceId>/action`.\n- **After an action, the page data is refetched.** Calls under `queries.` skip the refetch, which is what you want for polling.\n- Dates become ISO strings, bigint becomes a string, `Uint8Array` becomes `{ type: \"bytes\", byteLength }`. Class instances and cycles become `undefined`, so return plain objects.\n\n## Saving settings\n\nCall `onSaveConfig(config)` with the **whole** config object. The host writes `plaios.yaml`, reloads the runtime, and your page re-renders with the new values. `ConfigurationEditor` does all of this for you.\n\n## Styling\n\n`web/styles.css` is loaded once, next to your page:\n\n```css web/styles.css\n@import \"@plaios/microfrontend/styles.css\";\n@source \".\";\n```\n\nThat import gives you Tailwind with the host's theme tokens (`bg-background`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border`, `primary`...), so your page follows light and dark mode with no work. Prefix your own classes (`ha-`, `cron-`, `ws-`) so you never collide with another plugin.\n\n> **Tip** The page is not required to use `MicrofrontendFrame`. This guide does not: it is its own layout, inside the same contract."
  },
  {
    "id": "chat-components",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Chat components",
    "tagline": "Teach the model to draw. Live cards, inside its answers.",
    "markdown": "This is the part that feels like magic. Ask *\"show me the garage cameras\"* and three live camera cards appear in the answer. Ask for the news and a row of source cards slides in. The model did not output HTML. It wrote one line of a tiny language, and your plugin rendered it.\n\n## Two halves, one name\n\n**1. The capability declares the component** with `uiComponents()`. This is what the model learns:\n\n```ts src/ui-components.ts\nimport type { UIComponentSpec } from \"@plaios/core/domain\";\n\nexport const WEB_SOURCES: UIComponentSpec = {\n  name: \"WebSources\",\n  description: \"The sources of one web search as a row of cards. Use it right after search_web: WebSources(\\\"<id returned by search_web>\\\"). Never invent an id.\",\n  props: [{ name: \"searchId\", type: \"string\", description: \"The id field search_web returned.\" }],\n};\n```\n\n```ts src/index.ts\nuiComponents(): readonly UIComponentSpec[] {\n  return [WEB_SOURCES];\n}\n```\n\n**2. The web entry exports a renderer** with the same name, in `openui`:\n\n```tsx web/action/index.tsx\nimport type { OpenUIComponentModule } from \"@plaios/microfrontend\";\nimport { WEB_SOURCES } from \"../../src/ui-components.js\";\nimport { WebSources } from \"./chat/web-sources.js\";\n\nexport const openui: OpenUIComponentModule<SearchBridge, { searchId?: string }>[] = [\n  { ...WEB_SOURCES, component: WebSources },\n];\n```\n\nKeeping the spec in one React-free file, imported by both halves, means the model and the browser can never disagree about the name or the props.\n\n## What the renderer receives\n\n```ts @plaios/microfrontend\ncomponent: ComponentType<MicrofrontendProps<TData> & { args: TArgs; onOpen?(instanceId: string): void }>\n```\n\n- `args`: the props the model wrote, validated against your spec (`string`, `string[]`, `number`, `boolean`).\n- `data`: your live bridge data, the same object your page gets. The camera cards poll `data.queries.snapshot(...)`. The source cards call `data.queries.search(id)`.\n- `onOpen(instanceId)`: jump to your component page.\n\n## Writing specs the model uses well\n\n- **Describe when to use it, with an example call** in the description. `HomeCameras(\"garage\")` teaches more than a paragraph.\n- **Pass ids, not data.** `WebSources(searchId)` costs a handful of tokens, and the model cannot invent URLs. Store the data server-side (see [The database](#database)) and look it up in the renderer.\n- **Say whether a tool call is needed first.** \"It needs no tool call\" lets the model answer instantly.\n\n## The quieter sibling: `Chat`\n\nExport a `Chat` component from your web entry, and when the agent uses your capability in a turn, the web chat shows it as a small card under the answer, with an *Open* link to your page. Cron uses it to show its schedule right where you asked about it.\n\n> **Careful** Chat components only render in the web chat, and only for components that are enabled and have a data bridge (`microfrontendData`). Telegram and other text outputs never see them, so the answer text must stand on its own."
  },
  {
    "id": "ai-docs",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Docs for the agent",
    "tagline": "Write the manual once. The agent reads it exactly when it needs to.",
    "markdown": "Your `description` gets you chosen. Your `docs()` gets you used **well**.\n\nEvery agent loop has `read_capability_docs` (id `agent-os.docs`) loaded on every turn. When the model is unsure about your capability, or a call just failed, it reads your manual instead of guessing.\n\n## What the agent gets\n\n`read_capability_docs({ capabilityId: \"web.search\" })` returns markdown assembled by the host, in this order:\n\n1. `# search_web (web.search)` and your description\n2. **your `docs()`**\n3. your input schema\n4. your `examples`\n5. your configuration, with current values, and secrets shown only as *set* or *not set*\n6. your chat components, as `Name(prop?: type)` signatures\n\nSo **do not repeat** the schema, the config or the components in `docs()`. Spend the words on what only you know.\n\n## A great `docs()` has four parts\n\n```ts src/index.ts\ndocs(): string {\n  return `Searches the live web and returns ranked sources.\n\n**Use it** for anything that may have changed recently: news, prices, releases.\n**Don't use it** for things memory or another capability already knows.\n\n**Inputs**\n- query: write it like a search: {\"query\":\"Node.js 26 release date\"}\n- searchRecencyFilter for fresh topics: {\"query\":\"Serie A results\",\"searchRecencyFilter\":\"day\"}\n\n**Limits**: 30 s timeout. WEB_SEARCH_FAILED is retryable once.`;\n}\n```\n\n1. **When to use it, and when not**, naming the sibling to use instead.\n2. **How the inputs really behave**, with one to three example calls as JSON.\n3. **What to do after**: which chat component to show, what to tell the user.\n4. **Limits and failure codes**, and which ones are worth a retry.\n\n> **Tip** Write it for a smart colleague on their first day, not for a search engine. Short sentences, concrete examples, no marketing."
  },
  {
    "id": "ship",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Build, publish, install",
    "tagline": "From your laptop to the Store to a running agent.",
    "markdown": "PLAIOS installs plugins from an npm registry, a private Verdaccio by default. Publishing is just npm publishing.\n\n## 1. Build\n\n```bash terminal\npnpm build    # tsc: src and web compile into dist\npnpm test\n```\n\nCheck `files` in `package.json` includes everything the exports point to: `dist`, `web` (for `styles.css`), and `agent-os.package.yaml`. A package without its manifest installs as an empty box.\n\n## 2. Version\n\nBump `version` in **both** `package.json` and `agent-os.package.yaml`. Breaking a capability's input schema is a major change for the agent too.\n\n## 3. Publish\n\n```bash terminal\npnpm publish --registry http://<your-registry>:4873\n```\n\n`workspace:*` dependencies are rewritten to the exact versions in your workspace, so publish `@plaios/core` first if you changed it.\n\n## 4. Install\n\nFrom the **Store**, press **Install**. The host then:\n\n1. runs `pnpm add` for your package into `.agent-os/runtime`\n2. reads your manifest and checks every interface's page resolves (or uninstalls and tells you why)\n3. adds one component per interface to `plaios.yaml`, with id `<kind>:<package-id>` and your config defaults (interfaces with `startup.when: manual` are skipped)\n4. reloads the runtime\n\nOr pin it by hand:\n\n```yaml plaios.yaml\ncomponents:\n  - package: \"@plaios/weather\"\n    version: 0.1.0\n    interface: weather.action\n    instanceId: action:weather\n    config:\n      days: 3\n```\n\nOn boot the host installs any pinned version that is missing, so the same `plaios.yaml` reproduces the same agent on any machine.\n\n## Updating\n\nThe Store shows **Update** when the registry has a newer version than the one installed. Updating rewrites the pin and restarts the host.\n\n> **Tip** A registry that enforces a minimum release age (pnpm 11 does) will refuse a version published seconds ago. Add it to `minimumReleaseAgeExclude` in the runtime's `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, or wait."
  },
  {
    "id": "sharp-edges",
    "part": "Superpowers",
    "title": "Sharp edges, honestly",
    "tagline": "Everything the code does that you might not expect. Read it once, save a day.",
    "markdown": "Every system has corners. Here are ours, straight from the source, so none of them surprises you in production.\n\n- **`initialize()` is never called.** It is in the `Capability` type, but the host does not call it. Set up lazily on first use.\n- **Only `startup.when: manual` does anything.** It keeps an interface out of a Store install. `always` and `ifConfigured` behave the same today.\n- **`surfaces`, `permissions` and a yaml `capability:` block are ignored.** The capability manifest that counts is the one on your class.\n- **Values typed on the page beat coercion.** A `number` saved as `\"10\"` arrives as a string. Coerce in your constructor.\n- **A reload rebuilds everything.** Saving any config, installing, toggling: every component gets `close()` (or `stop()`) and is constructed again. Keep no state you cannot rebuild, and release every port and timer in `close`/`stop`.\n- **Shared instances keep the first options.** Interfaces sharing a `className` share one object, constructed with the first interface's options.\n- **One memory, one orchestrator, one agent loop.** Only the first of each is used. A second one is silently ignored.\n- **`outputChannel` must exist.** An orchestrator naming a missing output fails the whole turn.\n- **Bridge data must be plain.** Class instances and cycles in `microfrontendData()` turn into `undefined` on the way to the browser.\n- **Chat components need a data bridge.** No `microfrontendData()`, no chat components, even if `uiComponents()` returns some.\n- **Strict schemas.** Tool schemas go to the model in strict mode: list every property in `required` and use `null` for \"not given\".\n\n> **Tip** Found an edge that is not here? It is a bug in the guide or in the host. Both are worth a pull request."
  }
